---
// ===========================================================================
// contract.astro — the thesis, and a field drawn from a description.
//
// The page used to open with "Two members. That is the whole thing." — true,
// and an answer to a question nobody had asked yet. It opens with what a
// description is FOR, and the descriptor block below is the site's only
// evidence for the sentence on the home page: that a renderer can know what a
// field accepts before anyone types in it.
//
// The three stages in `drawn` were produced by running the zod resolver and
// buildInputProps, not typed out.
// ===========================================================================
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";

const drawn = `
// 1. the schema, which is the only place these are written
z.object({
  name:     z.string().min(3).max(20),
  role:     z.enum(["admin", "editor", "viewer"]),
  bio:      z.string().optional(),
  quantity: z.number().min(1).max(99),
})

// 2. what the resolver describes — before any value exists
{ path: "name",     kind: "string", isRequired: true,
  constraints: { minLength: 3, maxLength: 20 } }
{ path: "role",     kind: "string", isRequired: true, constraints: {},
  choices: [{ value: "admin", label: "admin" }, … ] }
{ path: "bio",      kind: "string", isRequired: false, constraints: {} }
{ path: "quantity", kind: "number", isRequired: true,
  constraints: { minimum: 1, maximum: 99 } }

// 3. what the input carries, with nobody writing it a second time
<input {...field.inputProps} />
// name     ->  required  minlength="3"  maxlength="20"
// quantity ->  required  min="1"        max="99"
`;

const resolver = `
type FormResolver<TSchema, T, TPath extends string = string> =
  (schema: TSchema) => FormAdapter<T, TPath>;

interface FormAdapter<T, TPath extends string = string> {
  readonly fields: readonly FormFieldDescriptor[];
  validate(root: unknown): MaybeAsync<readonly FormIssue[]>;
}
`;

const descriptor = `
interface FormFieldDescriptor {
  readonly path: string;          // "owner.name", "items[*].quantity"
  readonly kind: FormFieldKind;   // string | number | boolean | date | array | object | unknown
  readonly isRequired: boolean;
  readonly constraints: FormFieldConstraints;    // minimum, maxLength, pattern, format, step…
  readonly choices?: readonly FormFieldChoice[]; // present only on a closed field
}
`;

const types = `
const adapter = zodFormResolver(
  z.object({ owner: z.object({ email: z.string() }) })
);

type Values = FormValues<typeof adapter>;   // { owner: { email: string } }
type Paths  = FormPaths<typeof adapter>;    // "owner" | "owner.email"

const good: Paths = "owner.email";
const typo: Paths = "owner.emial";          // compile error
`;

const weight = `
// packages/resolver-zod — the only mention of zod in the built output.
import type * as z from "zod";
`;
---

<Base
  title="The contract — form-contract"
  description="A validator already knows a field is required, capped at twenty characters and one of four values. The contract is how it says so before anyone types — two members, and the value type survives them."
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold sm:text-4xl">The contract</h1>

  <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    <strong class="text-on-surface">A validator already knows that this field
    is required, capped at twenty characters and one of three values.</strong>{" "}
    The contract is how it says so <em>before</em> anyone types — which is the
    only moment a renderer can use it.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">What a descriptor draws</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    The required marker, the length bounds, the numeric range and a closed
    field's options come off the schema, so nobody writes them a second time in
    the JSX.
  </p>

  <Code code={drawn} />

  <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Stage 2 is what the resolver returns and stage 3 is what{" "}
    <code>inputProps</code> carries; both were produced by running them, not
    typed out. An attribute is emitted only when the schema declared the bound
    — <code>bio</code> declared nothing, so its input carries nothing.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">Two members</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    All of that is two members: a list of descriptors, and one function that
    judges a whole root.
  </p>

  <Code code={resolver} />

  <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    A resolver is a plain function, named at the call site. There is no
    registry, no vendor tag and no dispatch, because whoever writes the call
    already knows which validator they are using.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">One descriptor is one field</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    One entry per leaf, and an array is described once through a wildcard
    rather than once per row — because the number of rows is not known until
    there is a value, and the description has to be readable before one exists.
  </p>

  <Code code={descriptor} />

  <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Constraint values are carried <em>as declared</em>. A pattern stays a{" "}
    <code>RegExp</code>, because how a renderer spells one is that renderer's
    business.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">The type survives</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    The resolver hands the value type through, which is why a misspelt path is
    a compile error rather than an input that renders blank and is never
    validated.
  </p>

  <Code code={types} />

  <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Carrying the type is also what keeps the contract neutral about direction:
    a type-first validator passes the type its rules were written against, a
    schema-first one passes what its schema infers, and both arrive as the same
    form.{" "}
    <a class="text-primary underline underline-offset-4" href="/paths/"
      >How a path gets its type →</a
    >
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">Bring your own validator, and your own copy of it</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    The resolver never imports zod at run time — one <code>import type</code>,
    erased at build, and the compiled output does not mention it. So it cannot
    pin a version or pull in a second copy: zod is declared as a peer, and the
    one you already installed is the one it describes.
  </p>

  <Code code={weight} />

  <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    One resolver ships today and it is zod. The contract is two members, so
    writing another is a plain function — and{" "}
    <a class="text-primary underline underline-offset-4" href="/start/"
      >what each package gives you</a
    > is on the Start page.
  </p>
</Base>
